Create a network.

Only the name is required. The subnet defaults to the next free private /24,
this router takes the first address in it, and the interface defaults to the
one adopted interface that does not already carry a network. Every one of
those is overridable and none of them has to be typed.

Applying this writes the router's address to the interface. If you are
connected to this box over that interface, your session will drop — run it
with --dry-run first, which prints exactly what would change.

Usage:
  olr net add <name> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for add
      --member string   the interface the network lives on (default: the only adopted interface without one)
      --no-subnet       serve no IPv4 here, leaving the network to router advertisement alone
      --router string   this router's address on the network (default: the first address in the subnet)
      --subnet string   the network in CIDR form, e.g. 172.16.1.0/24 (default: the next free private /24)

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
